# Maintain architecture rules

When a change alters structure, contracts, or placement conventions, update `.cursor/rules/` in the **same** task. Stale rules waste tokens and cause wrong edits.

## Update when

- New/renamed folder under `src/`, `core/`, `tools/`, `concepts/`, `server/`
- Visual art direction / UI look conventions (`concepts/ART_DIRECTION.md`)
- New context, feature, event bus, or FSM state
- Feature isolation or `core/` project-agnostic boundary changes
- Scene-isolation / Resource-default / parent-override-via-events contract changes
- Editor-first UI policy changes (scene nodes vs programmatic UI)
- Target platforms (Mobile / Steam / HTML) or cross-platform playability rules change
- HFSM library semantics or JSON config conventions change (`core/lib/hfsm`, `hfsm-*.mdc`)
- Battle start payload / `IScene` / progress schema changes
- Level layout or `LevelsConfig` container IDs change
- Entry point or main-scene wiring changes
- Path-resolution convention (`class_name` / `@export` vs hardcoded `res://`)
- Server/Docker/Nakama conventions under `server/` (`server-backend.mdc`)
- Online identity feature under `src/features/online/`
- A convention in these rules is deliberately broken or replaced

## How

1. Edit the smallest relevant rule file (prefer patch over rewrite).
2. Keep each rule under ~50 lines; one concern per file.
3. Prefer paths + must/never over prose.
4. If a rule becomes wrong, fix or delete it — never leave contradictions.
5. After structural work, briefly confirm rules still match reality.

## Do not

- Duplicate the same map across many rule files
- Document one-off bugs or temporary hacks as architecture
- Expand rules with unused library details (e.g. unused FSM Conditional)
